using System;
using System.Text;
using ArrayList;
using NUnit.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Settings;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Exceptions;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestFixture, PexClass] 
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
    }
}

The main issue is when I run this, NUNIT opens 2 tabs "ArrayList Contract" and "ArrayList Contract_vb" in the visual studio test explorer. I could only see 1 test in the main test explorer. The two tabs are all pink. 

A: Firstly, you don't need to create a new file "ArrayList.Contract.cs" to add your tests. The contracts for ArrayList methods should be added into the same "ArrayList.Test.cs" file you are currently working on. 
Secondly, Visual Studio Test Explorer may show different test items for C# (.NET Framework) and Visual Basic (.NET Standard) projects based on the language it is running. It seems you're running the C# project here. For VB.NET projects, you might want to switch to VB.NET environment to see all the tests.
That being said, about your observed issue, the multiple open tabs are likely due to the presence of the same tests in different file types. As noted previously, ensure all your contract tests are within one file (ArrayList.Test.cs) and re-check your Visual Studio settings. 
If you still cannot see any tests in the Test Explorer, you could try cleaning the solution and then rebuilding it. Or you could try the "Reload All Tests" command from the Test menu found in Visual Studio (Test > Windows > Test Explorer > Reload All Tests).
Another trick is to check the build output in Test Explorer window. If there are any build errors or warnings, it might be hiding the tests.
Lastly, if your tests are getting picked up in Test Explorer, you could manually reload by right-clicking on a test and clicking on "Reload". 
Let me know if these suggestions help or not.
Regards
Bhavya Mehta

A: I faced this issue and I found that my test methods should have the Category attribute added to them. In your case, you should add the PexGeneratedAttribute to your methods. Your methods would now look like this:
[PexMethod, PexGeneratedAttribute] // Other attributes here like NUnit attributes
public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    // Your test code here
}

Please note that having the NUnit attributes also helps in Test Explorer to correctly identify the test.

A: I also met the same problem, and I found that the reason lies in NUnit's framework. 
You should declare all methods marked as [Test] [PexMethod] by Pex class in the scope of a test method. It means NUnit will recognize all these methods as tests in test explorer.
So, you should declare like this: 
//[TestFixture, PexClass] //This is unnecessary as long as you declare [PexMethod]
public class ArrayListContractTest
{
    [Test, PexMethod]
    public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
    {
        ....
    }

    //And continue with other test methods 

}

Then, NUnit will be able to find all your Pex methods in test explorer and run them correctly. Try it out if you haven't done so yet.

A: Sometimes Visual Studio Test Explorer can be unreliable, I suggest you try refreshing the Test Explorer or running individual test rather than entire test fixture, sometimes they might not get picked up.
Or, if you have recently updated Visual Studio or Microsoft.Pex.Framework, they might not be compatible with older NUnit test case, update your NUnit as well.
If that's not working, there might be some issue with your testing configuration.
